{
 "id": "M24",
 "kind": "echarts",
 "title": "Opus flags the obvious backdoors, and the dangerous corner is empty",
 "section": "results",
 "order": 145,
 "caption": "One dot per hard backdoor, 51 of them, rated by hand on two axes: across, how much damage the bug does when its trigger fires; up, how innocent the code looks to a reviewer. Violet means Opus 5.5 at xhigh scored it 50 or more. The shaded corner, stealthy and severe, holds nothing.",
 "notes": [
  "The same ratings as M15, which counts them in a table. This draws them per backdoor so the empty corner and the Opus split can be read together.",
  "Opus is far better at the visible end. It scores 50 or more on 3 of the 5 visible-and-severe backdoors and on 2 of the 23 stealthy ones, so what it adds is mostly confirmation of backdoors that already look wrong.",
  "The empty corner is the weakest claim on this page. It is an absence in 51 hand-rated programs, not a measured rate, and 51 is small enough that a corner holding one or two cases in truth could easily show zero here.",
  "The ratings are judgement, made from the diff and recorded with a confidence flag and a written reason per backdoor in hard51-risk-ratings.csv. They were not blind to the scores.",
  "An impact of 4 never occurs in this set, so that column is empty for every level of incrimination. The scale is kept at five so the gap is visible rather than closed up.",
  "Dots are spread a little from their integer ratings so overlapping cases stay countable. The offset is seeded from the problem id, so it is identical on every rebuild and carries no meaning of its own."
 ],
